{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\n56% of sampled fixes add a new mechanism (lock, knob, env var, gate script, fixture, helper, flag) vs 30% pure logic fixes (R2S1): the surface that produces future defect classes grows with every fix cycle. No unified detector exists (12a); per-kind precedents prove detectability (`check_config_reads.py`, `gen_env_registry.py`). The baseline is keyed per `(kind, name)` — never a scalar sum — per the ESLint `eslint-suppressions.json` shape (12c), so one kind cannot be traded against another.\n\n## Approach\n\n`scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py` detects, per repo STATE (state-diffing the baseline is simpler than diffing a change and equivalent), seven mechanism kinds.\n\n**The kinds PARTITION the surface: every definition site yields exactly ONE `(kind, name)` entry.** This is correctness, not tidiness. A boolean `rebar.toml` key would otherwise be counted twice — once as `config_key`, once as `feature_flag` — because the flag detector reads the same `_SECTIONS` dict, so one site would need two justifications and a per-kind marker could not express that. `feature_flag` therefore claims the boolean-coerced entries and `config_key` claims only the non-boolean remainder.\n\nLive counts, locked at introduction:\n\n| kind | detection | count |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lock` | AST `ClassDef` matching `.*Lock.*`, plus string literals matching `\\.lock$` | 26 |\n| `env_var` | `gen_env_registry.scan()`, filtered to `REBAR_*` | 132 |\n| `config_key` | `_config_sections._SECTIONS`, excluding boolean-coerced entries | 76 |\n| `feature_flag` | the boolean-coerced `_SECTIONS` entries (no dedicated flag registry exists) | 27 |\n| `ci_gate` | `scripts/check_*.py` glob, plus workflow `run:` steps | 280 (29 scripts + 251 run-steps) |\n| `autouse_fixture` | AST: `pytest.fixture(autouse=True)` decorators | 145 |\n| `test_helper` | `tests/_*.py` glob | 19 |\n\n**Baseline and modes.** `.github/mechanism-baseline.json`, shape `{\"schema_version\": 1, \"mechanisms\": {\"<kind>::<name>\": 1}}`, sorted keys, duplicate-member and UTF-8 rejection — a near-verbatim port of the complexity baseline's validated schema. Its four-bucket `compare()` (new / increased / active / stale) and `has_regression = new or increased` port unchanged; they are 16 proven lines.\n\n`check_complexity_baseline.py` exposes three commands — `--check`, `--update-stale`, `--lock`. This plan adopts the first two and omits `--lock`, which exists only because the complexity *threshold* is administrator-locked and a raised ceiling must be caught cross-branch. The mechanism baseline has no locked limit, so `--lock` would add ~135 LOC guarding nothing; it is the known port if that changes.\n\n**The justified-exception path is an in-tree marker parsed by this script**, exactly as `check_config_reads.py` consumes its own `# read-via:` markers:\n\n```\n# mechanism-ok: <kind> <name> — <reason or ticket id>\n```\n\nA blank reason is itself an error. Because the kinds partition, one site needs one marker naming one kind.\n\n**Where the marker attaches.** Three kinds are detected by filename glob or YAML structure and have no definition line, so placement is stated per detection shape; each detector scans exactly these locations:\n\n| detection shape | kinds | marker location |\n|---|---|---|\n| Python definition line | `lock` (class), `autouse_fixture`, `config_key`, `feature_flag` | on the definition line or the one immediately preceding it |\n| string literal | `lock` (`*.lock` names), `env_var` | on the literal's line or the one preceding it, in any attributed file |\n| filename glob | `ci_gate` (`scripts/check_*.py`), `test_helper` (`tests/_*.py`) | anywhere in the matched file's first 20 lines |\n| YAML step | `ci_gate` (workflow `run:` steps) | on the step's `- name:`/`run:` line or the one preceding it |\n\nA mechanism attributed to several files is admitted by a marker in any one; the diagnostic names every attributed file.\n\nAn earlier draft routed justification through \"the DET floor\". That was wrong and is dropped: `det_floor.py` (P1–P11) is the per-ticket plan-review gate dispatched by the orchestrator over a ticket description, is not in `make lint`, and cannot be consulted by a static tree-scanning script.\n\n**Structure.** Seven detectors in one file would breach the 800-LOC cap and the complexity-15 ceiling, so this ships as `scripts/_mechanism_delta/{baseline,compare,detect_code,detect_config,detect_ci}.py` with a thin entrypoint. Detectors split by INPUT SURFACE (AST / config registry / globs+YAML); dispatch is a flat `{kind: callable}` table, keeping the entrypoint near complexity 4. Portable: stdlib plus PyYAML, triggered by `make lint`, no CI-provider dependency.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: the seven detectors' name sets are pairwise disjoint on the current tree. Verified by a test asserting every pair has an empty intersection.\n- [ ] AC2: `config_key` excludes boolean-coerced `_SECTIONS` entries. Verified by a test asserting its live count is 76.\n- [ ] AC3: `feature_flag` claims exactly those boolean entries. Verified by a test asserting its live count is 27.\n- [ ] AC4: the script detects all seven kinds with zero false positives. Verified by `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exiting 0 on a clean tree.\n- [ ] AC5: `--check` exits non-zero when a new `(kind, name)` appears unjustified. Verified by a parameterized test seeding one new mechanism of each of the seven kinds.\n- [ ] AC6: a valid marker admits a new mechanism of a definition-line kind. Verified by a test seeding a marked `_SECTIONS` entry.\n- [ ] AC7: a valid marker admits a filename-glob kind. Verified by a test seeding a new `scripts/check_*.py` with its marker in the first 20 lines.\n- [ ] AC8: a valid marker admits a YAML-step kind. Verified by a test seeding a workflow `run:` step with a marker on the preceding line.\n- [ ] AC9: a blank-reason marker fails. Verified by a test seeding one.\n- [ ] AC10: mechanism removal passes. Verified by a test removing a baselined mechanism and asserting exit 0.\n- [ ] AC11: `--update-stale` refuses to write when `new>0` or `increased>0`. Verified by a test asserting the refusal and that the baseline is byte-identical afterwards.\n- [ ] AC12: `make lint` runs the check. Verified by a test asserting the `lint` target contains the invocation.\n- [ ] AC13: the ratchet and its seven kinds are documented beside the complexity ratchet. Verified by a test asserting the docs name all seven kind identifiers.\n- [ ] AC14: the marker placement table is documented in that section. Verified by a test asserting the docs name each of the four detection shapes.\n- [ ] AC15: every shipped module stays under the 800-LOC cap. Verified by the module-size gate.\n- [ ] AC16: every shipped function stays under the complexity-15 ceiling. Verified by `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py -v` — covers AC1–AC3 and AC5–AC14. Census counts are derived LIVE from the tree, never hardcoded, following `tests/unit/test_complexity_baseline.py`'s stated discipline.\n- `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` — exits 0 on the clean tree (AC4).\n- `make lint` — runs the gate beside `check_complexity_baseline.py --check` (AC12) and proves the additions clear the complexity ratchet (AC16).\n- `make typecheck` — the package type-checks.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py` (new; entrypoint plus dispatch table)\n- `scripts/_mechanism_delta/` (new package: `baseline.py`, `compare.py`, `detect_code.py`, `detect_config.py`, `detect_ci.py`)\n- `.github/mechanism-baseline.json` (new; locked at introduction)\n- `Makefile` (lint wiring after `check_complexity_baseline.py --check`)\n- `tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py` (new; the precedent `test_complexity_baseline.py` lives in `tests/unit/`)\n- `docs/` (ratchet note beside the complexity ratchet)"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788319637745479001,"uuid":"16f913b4-f21d-40fd-a61a-280bb3b2644f"}